**`ErrorBoundary.tsx`** — key decisions:

- **`getDerivedStateFromError`** stores the error in state to trigger the fallback render.
- **`componentDidCatch`** logs message + stack + component stack to `console.error`.
- **`reset`** clears the error, re-mounting children — the retry mechanism.
- **`fallback` prop** lets callers provide a custom UI; omit it for the built-in one.

**Usage:**

```tsx
// Default fallback
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(err, retry) => (
  <div>
    <p>{err.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

> Error boundaries only catch errors in **render / lifecycle methods** of child class components and hooks. They don't catch async errors (event handlers, `setTimeout`) — handle those with `try/catch` or a global `window.onerror` listener.
